{"abstract":"This study explores the relationship between the governance of architecturally embedded applications and their longevity in organizational use. Using a contingency-fit logic, it posits that alignment between application governance (distribution of decision rights) and architectural contingencies contributes to sustained durations of organizational use. Architectural contingencies include internal application modularity, application inter-connectedness, and governance of surrounding application clusters. Evidence for the governance-architecture fit hypothesis is derived from analyzing temporally ordered data about applications (n = 225, n = 498, n = 193) from organizations in the media, banking, and utilities sectors over five years. Using a two-step approach with ordinal regression, along with Cox proportional hazards and logistic regression models, our findings indicate that a fit between observed governance and the governance induced by architectural contingencies reduces the likelihood of application decommissioning. The study advances the fields of information systems (IS) governance and architecture by offering a micro-level perspective on the longevity of organizational IS use while also shedding light on the importance of governance choices aligned with unique architectural application properties.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Akbari, Karl","Fürstenau, Daniel","Winkler, Till J."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-729089"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Winkler, Till J."],"country":["Germany","Taiwan"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National Taiwan University of Science and Technology","University of Hagen"],"keyword":["Application discontinuance","Application governance","Application longevity","Contingency fit","Digital infrastructure","Information systems architecture","Modular systems","Survival Analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"governance and longevity of architecturally embedded applications","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/07421222.2023.2301169","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-729089","identifier":[{"id":"85185225215","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-6b95b5cd-f380-42b8-8be2-e60e4a057e2e"],"keyword":["Application discontinuance","Application governance","Application longevity","Contingency fit","Digital infrastructure","Information-systems architecture","Modular systems","Survival analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-29606FE309BBB3ACCD83D7A0F20745D1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/100942196/akbari_karl_et_al_governance_and_longevity_of_architecturally_embedded_applications_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/07421222.2023.2301169","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"tw","key":"national taiwan university of science and technology","loc":["Taiwan","Asia, East"],"name":"National Taiwan University of Science and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l