llow-up from a multicentre, open-label, randomised, phase 3 trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-097.52-803760","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The COVID 19 crisis has highlighted the key role of the public health service (PHS), with its approximately 375 municipal health offices involved in the pandemic response. Here, in addition to a lack of human resources, the insufficient digital maturity of many public health departments posed a hurdle to effective and scalable infection reporting and contact tracing. In this article, we present the maturity model (MM) for the digitization of health offices, the development of which took place between January 2021 and February 2022 and was funded by the German Federal Ministry of Health. It has been applied since the beginning of 2022 with the aim of strengthening the digitization of the PHS. The MM aims to guide public health departments step by step to increase their digital maturity to be prepared for future challenges. The MM was developed and evaluated based on qualitative interviews with employees of public health departments and other experts in the public health sector as well as in workshops and with a quantitative survey. The MM allows the measurement of digital maturity in eight dimensions, each of which is subdivided into two to five subdimensions. Within the subdimensions a classification is made on five different maturity levels. Currently, in addition to recording the digital maturity of individual health departments, the MM also serves as a management tool for planning digitization projects. The aim is to use the MM as a basis for promoting targeted communication between the health departments to exchange best practices for the different dimensions.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Eymann, Torsten","Fürstenau, Daniel","Gersch, Martin","Kauffmann, Anna Lina","Neubauer, Maria","Schick, Doreen","Schlömer, Nina","Schulte-Althoff, Matthias","Stark, Jeanette","von Welczeck, Laura"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384766"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin","Fraunhofer FIT","Free University of Berlin","TU Dresden"],"keyword":["Digital health applications","Digital health care","Digital maturity","Public health services"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"das reifegradmodell für den öffentlichen gesundheitsdienst ein instrument zur erfassung und verbesserung des digitalen reifegrades von deutschen gesundheitsämtern","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00103-022-03643-7","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384766","identifier":[{"id":"85145992997","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ed36f9d6-475b-4166-a429-d1e64a12998e"],"keyword":["Digital health applications","Digital health care","Digital maturity","Public health services"],"lang":"German","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-18570FB7C57F26B452449200FAC20CDA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00103-022-03643-7","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92843062/s00103_022_03643_7_2_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"fraunhofer fit","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Fraunhofer FIT","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"free university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Free University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"tu dresden","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"TU Dresden","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"de","key":"charité universitätsmedizin berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Torsten","key":"eymannpau","last":"Eymann","name":"Eymann, Torsten","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"ceef6d97-26fe-4163-9538-6685e98bce2e","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"gerschpau","last":"Gersch","name":"Gersch, Martin","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna Lina","key":"kauffmannpau","last":"Kauffmann","name":"Kauffmann, Anna Lina","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Maria","key":"neubauerpau","last":"Neubauer","name":"Neubauer, Maria","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Doreen","key":"schickpau","last":"Schick","name":"Schick, Doreen","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Nina","key":"schlömerpau","last":"Schlömer","name":"Schlömer, Nina","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthias","key":"schultealthoffpau","last":"Schulte-Althoff","name":"Schulte-Althoff, Matthias","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette","key":"starkpau","last":"Stark","name":"Stark, Jeanette","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura","key":"vonwelczeckpau","last":"von Welczeck","name":"von Welczeck, Laura","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00103-022-03643-7","issn":["14369990"],"journal":"Bundesgesundheitsblatt","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Bundesgesundheitsblatt"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85145992997"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ed36f9d6-475b-4166-a429-d1e64a12998e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Das Reifegradmodell für den Öffentlichen Gesundheitsdienst : Ein Instrument zur Erfassung und Verbesserung des digitalen Reifegrades von deutschen Gesundheitsämtern","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384766","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: Previous studies have revealed that users of symptom checkers (SCs, apps that support self-diagnosis and self-triage) are predominantly female, are younger than average, and have higher levels of formal education. Little data are available for Germany, and no study has so far compared usage patterns with people’s awareness of SCs and the perception of usefulness. Objective: We explored the sociodemographic and individual characteristics that are associated with the awareness, usage, and perceived usefulness of SCs in the German population. Methods: We conducted a cross-sectional online survey among 1084 German residents in July 2022 regarding personal characteristics and people’s awareness and usage of SCs. Using random sampling from a commercial panel, we collected participant responses stratified by gender, state of residence, income, and age to reflect the German population. We analyzed the collected data exploratively. Results: Of all respondents, 16.3% (177/1084) were aware of SCs and 6.5% (71/1084) had used them before. Those aware of SCs were younger (mean 38.8, SD 14.6 years, vs mean 48.3, SD 15.7 years), were more often female (107/177, 60.5%, vs 453/907, 49.9%), and had higher formal education levels (eg, 72/177, 40.7%, vs 238/907, 26.2%, with a university/college degree) than those unaware. The same observation applied to users compared to nonusers. It disappeared, however, when comparing users to nonusers who were aware of SCs. Among users, 40.8% (29/71) considered these tools useful. Those considering them useful reported higher self-efficacy (mean 4.21, SD 0.66, vs mean 3.63, SD 0.81, on a scale of 1-5) and a higher net household income (mean EUR 2591.63, SD EUR 1103.96 [mean US $2798.96, SD US $1192.28], vs mean EUR 1626.60, SD EUR 649.05 [mean US $1756.73, SD US $700.97]) than those who considered them not useful. More women considered SCs unhelpful (13/44, 29.5%) compared to men (4/26, 15.4%). Conclusions: Concurring with studies from other countries, our findings show associations between sociodemographic characteristics and SC usage in a German sample: users were on average younger, of higher socioeconomic status, and more commonly female compared to nonusers. However, usage cannot be explained by sociodemographic differences alone. It rather seems that sociodemographics explain who is or is not aware of the technology, but those who are aware of SCs are equally likely to use them, independently of sociodemographic differences. Although in some groups (eg, people with anxiety disorder), more participants reported to know and use SCs, they tended to perceive them as less useful. In other groups (eg, male participants), fewer respondents were aware of SCs, but those who used them perceived them to be more useful. Thus, SCs should be designed to fit specific user needs, and strategies should be developed to help reach individuals who could benefit but are not aware of SCs yet.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Kopka, Marvin","Scatturin, Lenhard","Napierala, Henry","Fürstenau, Daniel","Feufel, Markus A.","Balzer, Felix","Schmieding, Malte L."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695253"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin","Free University of Berlin","Humboldt University of Berlin","Technical University of Berlin"],"keyword":["Adoption","Awareness","Cross sectional study","Digital health","Health app","Mobile health","Survey","Symptom Checkers","Usage"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"characteristics of users and nonusers of symptom checkers in germany cross sectional survey study","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/46231","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695253","identifier":[{"id":"85163847266","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-72153ef7-b443-4db3-b706-93930b0cd7ac"],"keyword":["Adoption","Awareness","Cross-Sectional Study","Digital Health","Mobile health","Survey","Symptom Checkers","Usage","health app"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0BC82B89867D15F7A77426CC6BAC4AAF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/46231","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101715910/Symptom-Checker-PDF_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"charité universitätsmedizin berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"free university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Free University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"humboldt university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Humboldt University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2 3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Marvin","key":"kopkapau","last":"Kopka","name":"Kopka, Marvin","rank":1,"rolecode"