=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383080","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Standardized fall risk scores have not proven to reliably predict falls in clinical settings. Machine Learning offers the potential to increase the accuracy of such predictions, possibly vastly improving care for patients at high fall risks. We developed a boosting algorithm to predict both recurrent falls and the severity of fall injuries. The model was trained on a dataset including extensive information on fall events of patients who had been admitted to Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin between August 2016 and July 2020. The data were recorded according to the German expert standard for fall documentation. Predictive power scores were calculated to define optimal feature sets. With an accuracy of 74% for recurrent falls and 86% for injury severity, boosting demonstrated the best overall predictive performance of all models assessed. Given that our data contain initially rated risk scores, our results demonstrate that well trained ML algorithms possibly provide tools to substantially reduce fall risks in clinical care settings.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Rehfeld, Sven","Schulte-Althoff, Matthias","Schreiber, Fabian","Fürstenau, Daniel","Näher, Anatol-Fiete","Hauss, Armin","Köhler, Charlotte","Balzer, Felix"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-283480"],"event":{"end":"2022-05-30","place":"Nice, fr","start":"2022-05-27","title":"The 32nd Medical Informatics Europe Conference. MIE 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","Freie Universität Berlin","Robert Koch Institute"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the prediction of fall circumstances among patients in clinical care a retrospective observational study","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3233/SHTI220530","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-283480","identifier":[{"id":"85131107445","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-54f6573e-75b7-44a4-a665-f23dd78de125"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C70C2EBBAEF81868A70B0E1667DF7135"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3233/SHTI220530","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/72398098/rehfeld_et_al_the_prediction_of_fall_circumstances_among_patients_in_clinical_care_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"charite universitatsmedizin berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"robert koch institute","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Robert Koch Institute","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sven","key":"rehfeldpau","last":"Rehfeld","name":"Rehfeld, Sven","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthias","key":"schu