:"Andrea","key":"pinnapedh","last":"Pinna","name":"Pinna, Andrea","rank":15,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Bastien","key":"rancepedh","last":"Rance","name":"Rance, Bastien","rank":16,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Lucia","key":"sacchipedh","last":"Sacchi","name":"Sacchi, Lucia","rank":17,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Adrien","key":"ugonpedh","last":"Ugon","name":"Ugon, Adrien","rank":18,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Arriel","key":"benispedh","last":"Benis","name":"Benis, Arriel","rank":19,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Parisis","key":"gallospedh","last":"Gallos","name":"Gallos, Parisis","rank":20,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3233/shti220530","eisbn":["9781643682853"],"isbn":["9781643682846"],"issn":["09269630"],"pages":"575-576","place":"Amsterdam","publisher":"IOS Press","series":"Studies in Health Technology and Informatics","status":"p","subtitle":"Proceedings of MIE 2022","title":"Challenges of Trustable AI and Added-Value on Health"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85131107445"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-54f6573e-75b7-44a4-a665-f23dd78de125"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"The Prediction of Fall Circumstances Among Patients in Clinical Care : A Retrospective Observational Study","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-283480","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Aim The purpose of this paper is to investigate the implementation of value-based care principles in the context of frailty in the perioperative process, highlighting the importance of an integrative perspective considering medical and patient-centric outcomes as well as costs. Subject and methods This mixed-methods study employs a sequential design. Qualitative observational data were used to identify needs and barriers for implementing value-based principles, and quantitative methods were subsequently used to demonstrate the value of employing such an approach using data gathered from n = 952 patients. Propensity score matching was applied to identify the frailty-associated costs of the inpatient setting for n = 381 non-frail and n = 381 (pre-)frail patients, in particular considering patient-centric outcomes. Results The qualitative analysis identified three main challenges when implementing value-based principles in the context of perioperative care and frailty, namely challenges related to the cost, patient-centric, and integrative perspectives. In addressing these shortcomings, a quantitative analysis of a propensity score-matched sample of patients undergoing surgery shows additional frailty-associated costs of 3583.01 [1654.92; 5511.04] EUR for (pre-)frail patients and the influence of individual patient-centric attributes. Effect size Cohen’s d was 0.26. Conclusion The results demonstrate that frailty should be considered from an integrative perspective, taking cost, patient-centered outcomes, and medical outcomes into account simultaneously. The results also show the value of a research design which uses qualitative data for the identification of needs and barriers, as well as quantitative data for demonstrating the usefulness of the conceived value-based approach to perioperative care delivery.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Fürstenau, Daniel","Haneke, Hannah","Spies, Claudia","Walz, Tim","Schewina, Kai","Höft, Moritz","Mörgeli, Rudolf","Balzer, Felix"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-298776"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Fürstenau, Daniel"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","Freie Universität Berlin"],"keyword":["Barriers","Digital health solutions","Frailty","Integrative approach","Perioperative process","Value based health care"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tackling the frailty burden with an integravity value based approach results from a mixed methods study","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10389-021-01647-2","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-298776","identifier":[{"id":"85115090290","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000696446900001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["cbs-f4d52ad9-3369-4544-99cc-3c4bb0dc8879"],"keyword":["Barriers","Digital health solutions","Frailty","Integrative apporach","Integrative approach","Perioperative process","Value-based health care"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1AB98A0C777F6F8B7AF55F65930BB2DC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/75921873/f_rstenau_et_al_tackling_the_frailty_burden_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10389-021-01647-2","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"charite universitatsmedizin berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Hannah","key":"hanekepau","last":"Haneke","name":"Haneke, Hannah","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Claudia","key":"spiespau","last":"Spies","name":"Spies, Claudia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Tim","key":"walzpau","last":"Walz","name":"Walz, Tim","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Kai","key":"schewinapau","last":"Schewina","name":"Schewina, Kai","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Moritz","key":"höftpau","last":"Höft","name":"Höft, Moritz","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rudolf","key":"mörgelipau","last":"Mörgeli","name":"Mörgeli, Rudolf","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Felix","key":"balzerpau","last":"Balzer","name":"Balzer, Felix","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10389-021-01647-2","issn":["17413842","17413850"],"issn_org":["21981833"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Public Health","pages":"99-110","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Journal of Public Health","title_org":"Journal of Public Health: From Theory to Practice","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115090290"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-f4d52ad9-3369-4544-99cc-3c4bb0dc8879"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Tackling the Frailty Burden with an Integravity Value-Based Approach : Results from a Mixed-Methods Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-298776","year":"2022","yearsub":"2021"}
{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Witte, Anne-Katrin","Gubser, Rahel","Winkler, Till J.","Fürstenau, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383806"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Data sharing","Digital health care","Digital platforms"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"data connections in health startup ecosystems grounded theory study","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383806","ids":["itu-ae81d901-edc9-48bf-bcca-cbf106950d40"],"keyword":["Data sharing","Digital health care","digital platforms"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-0E8DE9F7C92D62071F6C9838CF397EB7"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne-Katrin","key":"wittepau","last":"Witte","name":"Witte, Anne-Katrin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rahel","key":"gubserpau","last":"Gubser","name":"Gubser, Rahel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Till J.","key":"winklerpau","last":"Winkler","name":"Winkler, Till J.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"ceef6d97-26fe-4163-9538-6685e98bce2e","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ae81d901-edc9-48bf-bcca-cbf106950d40"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Data Connections in Health Startup Ecosystems: Grounded Theory Study","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383806","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Introduction: Shared decision-making (SDM) is considered to be a promising approach to enable person-centredness and tailored treatment pathways, according to persons’ individual needs. Foremost, this concerns frail and elderly patients, who suffer from multiple health issues and increased vulnerability, requiring individualized healthcare. SDM and person-centred projects are being conducted and policy and regulatory settings are being implemented worldwide. However, little is known about facilitators and barriers for implementing SDM into clinical practice for the specific needs of frail and elderly patients. Objective and Method: We aim to gain a comprehensive overview over original studies on perceived facilitators and barriers for the adoption of shared decision-making within the clinical setting by elderly and frail patients and clinical physicians. Our objective is twofold: First, we aim at collecting and understanding facilitators and barriers addressed by elderly and frail patients and clinical physicians. Second, we aim at understanding the underlying approaches and methods employed in respective studies. We conducted a scoping review, following the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-analyses extension for Scoping Reviews (PRISMA-ScR). Based on the databases PubMed, Medline and CINAHL, we identified and reviewed 11 articles. Results: For the first research question, the articles indicate that patients want to participate in decision-making, fostering autonomy; while communication barriers, asymmetric power relationships between patients and clinical physicians and a lack of patients’ health literacy remain crucial barriers. For the second research question, quantitative and qualitative studies are equally applied. Second, research on perceived facilitators and barriers for the adoption of shared decision-making within the clinical setting by elderly and frail patients and clinical physicians are almost exclusively conducted after participation in a SDM consultation. Third, the evaluation of the conceptual approaches demonstrates that the selected articles are primarily concerned with the collection of determinants that enable or impede the implementation and adoption SDM, without employing a distinct theoretical framework for explaining these determinants and assessing underlying relationships. Forth, the selected studies mainly used surveys and interviews. Conclusion: Overall, we identified only a limited number of articles addressing patient and clinical physician perceptions on facilitators and barriers for SDM. These findings further imply that SDM research concerning elderly and frail patients should become more encompassing by employing research that incorporate theory-based qualitative analysis, patient and clinician development of SDM perceptions (before and after SDM consultation), and observations of SDM consultations. Observations are particularly relevant as these remain as a black box. Implications: We suggest conducting a qualitative study that examines facilitators and barriers of clinical physicians and patients both prior to and post to SDM consultation, in combination with a non-participatory observation of actual SDM consultations. The observation of practice is indispensable to enable a thorough understanding of SDM and its’ application.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Vogel, Amyn","Fürstenau, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-298564"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Freie Universität Berlin"],"keyword":["Healthcare innovation","Innovation adoption","Shared decision making"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"shared decision making a vehicle for participation and individualized clinical care pathways a scoping review on patients and physicians perceived facilitators and barriers for shared decision making for frail and elderly patients in the clinical setting","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5334/ijic.ICIC22012","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-298564","ids":["cbs-f2bf897a-ed8d-4e3f-bbbc-8acc8ce41f60"],"keyword":["Healthcare innovation","Innovation adoption","Shared decision making","Sociology of health"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-4D476FF78C517D10FF93F520F125B379"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5334/ijic.ICIC22012","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Amyn","key":"vogelpau","last":"Vogel","name":"Vogel, Amyn","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5334/ijic.icic22012","issn":["15684156"],"issue":"Annual Conference Supplement","journal":"International Journal of Integrated Care","publisher":"Ubiquity Press","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Integrated Care","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-f2bf897a-ed8d-4e3f-bbbc-8acc8ce41f60"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Shared Decision-making : A Vehicle for Participation and Individualized Clinical Care Pathways? A Scoping Review on Patients’ and Physicians’ Perceived Facilitators and Barriers for Shared Decision-making for Frail and Elderly Patients in the Clinical Setting","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-298564","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The rise of digital-themed research in information systems (IS) and other disciplines is signaling the dawn of a new era and an opportunity for IS to position itself as a reference discipline. Emerging digital-themed studies have argued that the logic of theorizing about IT does not necessarily translate to the digital – yet we have limited theoretical explanations and toolkits for this. We propose a theory of digital practices to explain the unit of “doing”, that is, what transpires in attaining a digital-themed objective. We present digital practices as a conceptual bridge that allows for interdisciplinary conversation around objective-driven digital-themed research such as digital – transformation, entrepreneurship, innovation, among others.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Baiyere, Abayomi","Fürstenau, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-296581"],"event":{"end":"2022-08-09","place":"Seattle, us","start":"2022-08-05","title":"The Academy of Management Annual Meeting 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Baiyere, Abayomi"],"keyword":["AOM Seattle 2022","AOM annual meeting proceedings 2022","Best paper"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"digital practices","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-296581","ids":["cbs-df23bcfe-7e97-407b-8606-41a0ec87cd84"],"keyword":["AOM Seattle 2022","AOM annual meeting proceedings 2022","Best paper"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-371DD3EE66F509887342FFA2FA4F3DF9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Abayomi","key":"baiyerepau","last":"Baiyere","locid":"5df73b16-ee57-4a45-a43f-9e6eb629180b","name":"Baiyere, Abayomi","orcid":"0000-0002-2211-4960","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Sonja","key":"tanejapedh","last":"Taneja","name":"Taneja, Sonja","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5465/ambpp.2022.103","issn":["00650668"],"place":"Briarcliff Manor, NY","publisher":"Academy of Management","series":"Academy of Management Proceedings","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Eighty-second Annual Meeting of the Academy of Management"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85176551719","2-s2.0-85188097576","2-s2.0-85208724517"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-df23bcfe-7e97-407b-8606-41a0ec87cd84"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Digital Practices","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-296581","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Shared decision-making (SDM) is an approach to enable patient-centeredness and tailored treatment pathways, within the perioperative setting. This could benefit frail and elderly patients, who suffer from multiple health issues and increased surgical vulnerability and postoperative complications, requiring individualized healthcare. However, little is known about facilitators and barriers to implementing SDM into clinical practice for the specific requirements of frail and elderly patients. The objective is to gain a comprehensive overview of original studies on facilitators and barriers to SDM perceived by clinical healthcare personnel and patients to SDM within the perioperative setting. Our aim is twofold: First, we aim at collecting and understanding facilitators and barriers regarding SDM addressed by elderly and frail patients and clinical healthcare personnel. Second, we aim at understanding the underlying approaches and methods employed in respective studies. METHODS: We conduct a scoping literature review, based on the databases MEDLINE, Embase, CINAHL, and Web of Science and report results with PRISMA-ScR. We follow the methodological framework developed by Arksey and O'Malley and the recommendations by the Joanna Briggs Institute. Restrictions relate to language: only English, French, and German language articles are considered. We developed a data charting template to organize the data extraction. We decided to conduct a scoping review to better understand key concepts and the implementation of SDM and to identify the evidence for barriers and facilitators of SDM as perceived by patients and clinical healthcare personnel. DISCUSSION: To our knowledge, this is the first scoping review to address SDM for elderly and frail patients within the perioperative setting. A preliminary search has been conducted. After removing all duplicates, we found 984 results. We assessed that a sufficient body of literature exists to conduct this scoping review. The scoping review will provide a map of the research on facilitators and barriers perceived by clinical healthcare personnel and patients regarding SDM for perioperative care. The results are meant to support researchers and clinical healthcare personnel, in determining further research initiatives and the implementation of SDM within perioperative care for elderly and frail patients. Research gaps and future directions will be addressed in the forthcoming review.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Vogel, Amyn","Guinemer, Camille","Fürstenau, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-297616"],"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Vogel, Amyn"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","Freie Universität Berlin"],"keyword":["Clinical personnel","Perioperative Care","Shared decision making"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"barriers and facilitators to shared decision making for frail and elderly patients within the perioperative setting a scoping review protocol","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-1549153/v1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-297616","ids":["cbs-e9779fd7-13f6-4946-8857-d9db69e8b782"],"keyword":["Clinical personnel","Elderly and frail paitents","Elderly and frail patients","Perioperative care","Shared decision-making"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-F6EA9BC0713D753AEAEF7B8ED07B6817"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-1549153/v1","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/71952306/amyn_vogel_et_al_barriers_and_facilitators_to_shared_decision_making_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"charite universitatsmedizin berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Amyn","key":"vogelpau","last":"Vogel","name":"Vogel, Amyn","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Camille","key":"guinemerpau","last":"Guinemer","name":"Guinemer, Camille","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-1549153/v1","pages":"20","place":"Durham, NC","publisher":"Research Square","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-e9779fd7-13f6-4946-8857-d9db69e8b782"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Barriers and Facilitators to Shared Decision-making for Frail and Elderly Patients within the Perioperative Setting : A Scoping Review Protocol","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-297616","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We examine how firm revenue scales with labor for revenue-per-employee (RPE) and is moderated by firm-level AI investment. We compare AI start-ups, in which AI provides a competitive advantage, with digital platforms and service start-ups. We use propensity score matching to explain the scaling of start-ups and find evidence for sublinear scaling intensity for revenue as a function of labor. Our study suggests similar scaling intensities between AI and service start-ups, while platform start-ups produce higher scaling intensities. We show that an increase in employee counts is associated with major revenue increases for platform start-ups, while increases were modest for service and AI start-ups.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Schulte-Althoff, Mattias","Fürstenau, Daniel","Moo Lee, Gene","Rothe, Hannes","Kauffman, Robert J."],"cuid":["S02-086.22-031150"],"event":{"end":"2022-12-16","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2022-12-14","title":"The 32nd Workshop on Information Technologies and Systems","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, South","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Canada","Germany","Singapore"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin","Free University of Berlin","Freie Universität Berlin","Institut commercial de Nancy Business School","Sauder School of Business","Singapore Management University","University of British Columbia"],"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Digital platforms","Economics","Labor market","Startup"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what fuels growth a comparative analysis of the scaling intensity of ai start ups","src":["cbs","itu"],"src-cod