{"abstract":"The literature on healthcare management and innovation has long noted that shared decision-making (SDM), a practice of organizing joint decisions between healthcare professionals and patients, should improve healthcare outcomes by increasing patient engagement and autonomy, and thus fostering patient-centeredness and equality. While SDM projects are increasingly implemented across Europe and worldwide, the diffusion of the practice remains partial. The healthcare management and innovation literature explores SDM trough the underlying assumption that its diffusion constraints result from an information problem, implying objective criteria and rational behavior. The purpose of this research is to conduct a study on the social construction of SDM and underlying rationales using the case of one of the largest healthcare markets worldwide – Germany. To capture the complexity of SDM, a frame analysis is conducted on its medial representations. Media are both influential in shaping public opinion as well as generating public discourses This enables to elaborate the different facets of the construct, to capture inherent patterns and to explore the consequences for the acceptance and diffusion of SDM in Germany. Three facilitating and three obstructive frames were assessed. The polarities of these findings range from the questioning of one's own decision-making authority to the perception of individual competence and decision-making agency. Moreover, this study reflects on how physicians’ and patients’ role for SDM is conceived. Regarding physicians these alternate between the perception of the ‘demigod in white’ and the loss of decision-making authority. Regarding the patients these alternate between the perception of the ‘layman’ and the competent patient, eager to participate in decision-making.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Vogel, Amyn","Fürstenau, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-286574"],"event":{"acronym":"AOM Annual Meeting","end":"2021-08-04","place":"Virtual, Online","start":"2021-07-29","title":"The Academy of Management Annual Meeting 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Freie Universität Berlin"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards patient centeredness media frames on shared decision making for healthcare treatment","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-286574","ids":["cbs-74eebab1-0271-4356-9599-627670bbf00d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-8BFB352A47C78C8FA24AF4E3D11ECEA2"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Amyn","key":"vogelpau","last":"Vogel","name":"Vogel, Amyn","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Sonia","key":"tanejapedh","last":"Taneja","name":"Taneja, Sonia","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5465/ambpp.2021.11549abstract","issn":["00650668"],"place":"Briarcliff Manor, NY","publisher":"Academy of Management","series":"Academy of Management Proceedings","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Eighty-first Annual Meeting of the Academy of Management"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-74eebab1-0271-4356-9599-627670bbf00d"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Towards Patient-Centeredness? : Media Frames on Shared Decision-Making for Healthcare Treatment","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-286574","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) is a severe neurological disease; as a result, patient care requires a complex network of specialists and equipment providers. Despite best intentions , the organizational and regulatory structures in Germany have not been geared towards efficient and effective coordination of care for patients and their caregivers. This situation calls for innovation of care procurement and provision. APST is a multi-sided, patient-centric platform, addressing the coordination of ALS care by combining case management and platform-based service/equipment exchanges. Over time two feedback cycles, quality of platform service and of care, as well as two research cycles, evidence-based care and medical research, have been established and the platform has become an infrastructure for ALS care, exemplifying social entrepreneurship for digital innovation in patient care. We use an interorganizational configuration lens (Lyytinen and Dams-gaard 2011) to reconstruct how the organizing logic shaped four components of APST (case management, transaction, information, and research platform).","author_count":9,"contributors":["Fürstenau, Daniel","Auschra, Carolin","Klein, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-276425"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","end":"2020-12-16","place":"Virtual, Online, in","start":"2020-12-13","title":"2020 International Conference on Information Systems - Making Digital Inclusive: Blending the Local and the Global, ICIS 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Einstein Center Digital Future","Freie Universität Berlin","University of Munster"],"keyword":["ALS","Configuration analysis","Interprofessional collaboration","Multi sided platform","Social entrepreneurship"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a configuration approach to multi sided platforms in healthcare an als platform case","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://aisel.aisnet.org/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1092&context=icis2020","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-276425","ids":["cbs-0b743f3d-03aa-4329-9d66-ed933e44b93c"],"keyword":["ALS","Care innnovation","Care innovation","Configuration analysis","Interprofessional collaboration","Multi-sided platform","Social entrepreneurship"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-63D541D46F979B763535C1DDA0455566"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"einstein center digital future","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Einstein Center Digital Future","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"university of munster","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Munster","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Carolin","key":"auschrapau","last":"Auschra","name":"Auschra, Carolin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"kleinpau","last":"Klein","name":"Klein, Stefan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Joey","key":"georgepedh","last":"George","name":"George, Joey","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Souren","key":"paulpedh","last":"Paul","name":"Paul, Souren","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Rahul","key":"depedh","last":"De'","name":"De', Rahul","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Elena","key":"karahannapedh","last":"Karahanna","name":"Karahanna, Elena","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Suprateek","key":"sarkerpedh","last":"Sarker","name":"Sarker, Suprateek","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Gal","key":"oestreichersingerpedh","last":"Oestreicher-Singer","name":"Oestreicher-Singer, Gal","rank":9,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000033"],"place":"Atlanta, GA","publisher":"Association for Information Systems. AIS Electronic Library (AISeL)","series":"Proceedings of the International Conference on Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 41st International Conference on Information Systems (ICIS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-0b743f3d-03aa-4329-9d66-ed933e44b93c"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"A Configuration Approach to Multi-Sided Platforms in Healthcare : An ALS Platform Case","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-276425","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Background: Telemedicine has been deployed to address issues in intensive care delivery, as well as to improve outcome and quality of care. Implementation of this technology has been characterized by high variability. Tele-intensive care unit (ICU) interventions involve the combination of multiple technological and organizational components, as well as interconnections of key stakeholders inside the hospital organization. The extensive literature on the benefits of tele-ICUs has been characterized as heterogeneous. On one hand, positive clinical and economical outcomes have been shown in multiple studies. On the other hand, no tangible benefits could be detected in several cases. This could be due to the diverse forms of organizations and the fact that tele-ICU interventions are complex to evaluate. The implementation context of tele-ICUs has been shown to play an important role in the success of the technology. The benefits derived from tele-ICUs depend on the organization where it is deployed and how the telemedicine systems are applied. There is therefore value in analyzing the benefits of tele-ICUs in relation to the characteristics of the organization where it is deployed. To date, research on the topic has not provided a comprehensive overview of literature taking both the technology setup and implementation context into account. Objective: We present a protocol for a scoping review of the literature on telemedicine in the ICU and its benefits in intensive care. The purpose of this review is to map out evidence about telemedicine in critical care in light of the implementation context. This review could represent a valuable contribution to support the development of tele-ICU technologies and offer perspectives on possible configurations, based on the implementation context and use case. Methods: We have followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses extension for Scoping Reviews (PRISMA-ScR) checklist and the recommendations of the Joanna Briggs Institute methodology for scoping reviews. The scoping review and subsequent systematic review will be completed by spring 2021. Results: The preliminary search has been conducted. After removing all duplicates, we found 2530 results. The review can now be advanced to the next steps of the methodology, including literature database queries with appropriate keywords, retrieval of the results in a reference management tool, and screening of titles and abstracts. Conclusions: The results of the search indicate that there is sufficient literature to complete the scoping review. Upon completion, the scoping review will provide a map of existing evidence on tele-ICU systems given the implementation context. Findings of this research could be used by researchers, clinicians, and implementation teams as they determine the appropriate setup of new or existing tele-ICU systems. The need for future research contributions and systematic reviews will be identified.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Guinemer, Camille","Boeker, Martin","Weiss, Björn","Fürstenau, Daniel","Balzer, Felix","Poncette, Akira-Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-285857"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Balzer, Felix"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","Einstein Center Digital Future","Freie Universität Berlin","University of Freiburg"],"keyword":["Critical care","Implementation","Intensive care","Intensive care unit","Scoping review","Tele ICU","Telemedicine"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"telemedicine in intensive care units protocol for a scoping review","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/19695","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-285857","identifier":[{"id":"85100150467","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-6d3b1d26-7c5d-44f5-b2dc-2b0b9526c8fd"],"keyword":["Critical care","Implementation","Intensive care","Intensive care unit","Scoping review","Tele-ICU","Telemedicine"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1A7D294532591888E05E0D431E784DD3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/19695","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/69575432/camille_guinemer_et_al_telemedicine_in_intensive_care_units_publishersversion.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"charite universitatsmedizin berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charite - Universitatsmedizin Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"university of freiburg","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Freiburg","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"de","key":"einstein center digital future","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Einstein Center Digital Future","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Camille","key":"guinemerpau","last":"Guinemer","name":"Guinemer, Camille","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"boekerpau","last":"Boeker","name":"Boeker, Martin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Björn","key":"weisspau","last":"Weiss","name":"Weiss, Björn","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4 5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Felix","key":"balzerpau","last":"Balzer","name":"Balzer, Felix","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Akira-Sebastian","key":"poncettepau","last":"Poncette","name":"Poncette, Akira-Sebastian","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2196/19695","issn":["19290748"],"issue":"12","journal":"JMIR Research Protocols","paperid":"e19695","publisher":"JMIR Publications Inc.","publisher_org":"J M I R Publications, Inc.","status":"p","title":"JMIR Research Protocols","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85100150467"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-6d3b1d26-7c5d-44f5-b2dc-2b0b9526c8fd"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Telemedicine in Intensive Care Units : Protocol for a Scoping Review","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-285857","year":"2020","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Mobile health startups develop innovative, sensor-based solutions that continuously collect health data. To generate added value from these large amounts of data, an integration of the solutions into the healthcare system is essential. In this context, the collaboration between interdependent healthcare stakeholders is required which can be enabled by structures considered as digital ecosystems. To understand the conditions for ecosystem participation, more specifically the incentives and disincentives for data openness, we conducted 30 interviews with four healthcare stakeholder groups in Germany and analyzed the data using a Grounded Theory approach. As a result, we developed a conceptual model that describes the integration of mobile sensor-based health solutions into digital health ecosystems. Thereby, we improve the understanding of incentives and disincentives for data openness on the collective ecosystem level, the ecosystem-stakeholder-group level, and the individual user level. Practically, we contribute by outlining important market entry barriers for mobile health startups.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Witte, Anne-Katrin","Fürstenau, Daniel","Zarnekow, Ruediger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-288986"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","end":"2020-12-16","place":"Virtual, Online, in","start":"2020-12-13","title":"2020 International Conference on Information Systems - Making Digital Inclusive: Blending the Local and the Global, ICIS 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Einstein Center Digital Future","Freie Universität Berlin","Technische Universität Berlin"],"keyword":["Data openness","Digital health ecosystem","Mobile sensor based health technology"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"digital health ecosystems for sensor technology integration a qualitative study on the paradox of data openness","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://aisel.aisnet.org/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1089&context=icis2020","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-288986","ids":["cbs-8f200da3-bb7b-4ceb-a9ec-afdd4759830d"],"keyword":["Data openness","Digital health ecosystem","Mobile sensor-based health technology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3E934CD272C706627084CD792E044904"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technische universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technische Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"einstein center digital future","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Einstein Center Digital Future","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne-Katrin","key":"wittepau","last":"Witte","name":"Witte, Anne-Katrin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3 4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Daniel","key":"fürstenaupau","last":"Fürstenau","locid":"e1b78deb-bdff-40e4-935b-f1a97937809c","name":"Fürstenau, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-8490-7707","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ruediger","key":"zarnekowpau","last":"Zarnekow","name":"Zarnekow, Ruediger","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Joey","key":"georgepedh","last":"George","name":"George, Joey","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Souren","key":"paulpedh","last":"Paul","name":"Paul, Souren","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Rahul","key":"depedh","last":"De'","name":"De', Rahul","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Elena","key":"karahannapedh","last":"Karahanna","name":"Karahanna, Elena","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Suprateek","key":"sarkerpedh","last":"Sarker","name":"Sarker, Suprateek","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Gal","key":"oestreichersingerpedh","last":"Oestreicher-Singer","name":"Oestreicher-Singer, Gal","rank":9,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000033"],"place":"Atlanta, GA","publisher":"Association for Information Systems. AIS Electronic Library (AISeL)","series":"Proceedings of the International Conference on Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 41st International Conference on Information Systems (ICIS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-8f200da3-bb7b-4ceb-a9ec-afdd4759830d"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Digital Health Ecosystems for Sensor Technology Integration : A Qualitative Study on the Paradox of Data Openness","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-288986","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"This paper reports on several studies in the context of implementing the humanoid social robot Pepper in a financial institution. The results show that the robot can affect the boundary relations between the roles of customer and service worker differently from common-sense expectations. While employees initially feared to be automated away by the robot, the results suggest that the relationship is more likely to change through an emotional bonding to the robot being projected to the company deploying it. Therefore, the robot might, at least partially, assume the role of the service worker as an ambassador of the company, which could recede more into the background in this regard. We discuss the implications of our findings in the context of current literature on the changing boundary relations through robot innovations.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Volpe, Gabriella","Schulte-Althoff, Matthias","Dillmann, David","Maurer, Emmanuelle","Niedenzu, Yannic","Schließer, Philipp","Fürstenau, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-296610"],"event":{"acronym":"IFIP WG86","end":"2020-12-11","place":"Online/Zoom - managed by University of Oslo Computing Centre","start":"2020-12-10","title":"IFIP Joint Working Conference 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Fürstenau, Daniel"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Einstein Center Digital Future","Freie Universität Berlin","Technische Universität Berlin"],"keyword":["Actor network theory","Anthropomorphism","Attachment","Boundary relations","Emotions","Humanoid robots","Intelligence"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"humanoid social robots and the reconfiguration of customer service","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/67886671/gabriella_volpe_et_al_humanoid_social_robots_acceptedversion.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-296610","identifier":[{"id":"85097815428","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-df60ba2a-6bf0-458a-a9ad-aaaea99515b0"],"keyword":["Actor-network theory","Anthropomorphism","Attachment","Boundary relations","Emotions","Humanoid robots","Intelligence"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BAFA2EDC1833AA59A47459963BFC55DD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/67886671/gabriella_volpe_et_al_humanoid_social_robots_acceptedversion.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"freie universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Freie Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technische universität berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technische Universität Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"einstein center digital future","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Einstein Center Digital Future","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gabriella","key":"volpepau","last":"Volpe","name":"Volpe, Gabriella","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corr